[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '90% of the AI influencers you see look obviously fake and they get roasted in the comments every single time. But there\'s a way most people still aren\'t aware of that can make your AI characters look like real people. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So in this video I\'ll walk you through the entire process and break it down into three simple steps on how to do it yourself and [music] stand out with an influencer that actually looks real. And the very first step [music] decides whether my character looks the same'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'across two different posts. So the first thing I do for every influencer is build a character sheet and that\'s what keeps each one looking like the same person every time I generate them. I\'m making three completely different influencers'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in this video and I\'ll start with the one that looks like me. The whole build runs inside one platform called OpenArt and I\'ll use it deliberately. It\'s the one place that gives me every image model and every video model together'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is the whole reason I can run the test at the end of this video without jumping between five [music] separate tools. Once I\'m inside I\'ll open the image tab on the left and I\'ll make sure NanoBanana 2 is selected as [music] the'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model because it\'s one of the best image generators out there. But if you want you can also go with GPT Image 2 since they [music] work pretty similarly. Then in the settings I\'ll set the output to 16 by 9 and 4K. For the one that looks'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like me I\'ll upload three real photos of myself into the reference box which makes this [music] an image to image generation and that\'s the step almost nobody bothers with. Most people describe a face from scratch [music] and'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let the model invent someone and it always tries to make a perfect symmetrical face that looks obviously AI no matter how sharp the skin is. So instead of asking for realistic skin [music] which just gets you a generic'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'version I\'ll name the real imperfections. And for this one that means keeping her actual facial asymmetry and the real visible pores across her skin. [music] A named flaw is what turns a good render into to'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specific person who looks like they actually exist. The prompt itself is one [music] sheet split into four panels from a full-body shot down to a tight macro on the face. And that macro is the panel [music] everything else relies on'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'later. I\'ll paste in the first prompt and see how it turns out. And it genuinely looks like me from every angle, which is exactly what [music] I wanted. By the way, every prompt I\'ll use in this video is linked in the description, so you can copy them'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] and follow along. Then I\'ll build the other two the same way just from a description instead of photos, so those two run as text to image. May is my skin care influencer, and she\'s meant to look genuinely pretty but still real.'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So I\'ll keep the real skin and name her specific marks, like the little beauty mark under her eye and a freckle near her temple. Then I\'ll paste in the next prompt, hit generate, and [music] see how it handles that one. This one also'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looks very realistic. Now I\'ll go with Mateo, who\'s a traveler, an ordinary-looking guy. So I\'ll lean into the imperfections and make sure his skin is rough with stubble, a bit of sun damage, and follow the same rules. And'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'honestly, this one turned out the best of the three so far because his overall face looks very realistic. Now [music] with all three sheets done, instead of having a clearly generated AI character, [music] I\'ve got three real characters.'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But a character sheet still doesn\'t show me how this person actually looks [music] out in a real environment. Now the next step is to bring these three to life by making them look like they\'re just recording their own content. The'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole goal here is to make each shot look like a real phone photo. And that means making it look a little bit wrong on purpose because a clean, perfect image is the single biggest thing that makes it feel AI. [music] So for each of'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the three, I\'ll make two photos from two different moments of their day. I\'m still in the same image generator on Nano Banana 2, and I\'ll feed the character sheet back in as the reference. And the face carries straight over instead of getting reinvented from'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scratch. [music] The one setting I\'ll actually change is the aspect ratio. I\'ll switch it to 9 by 16 for the ones that post vertically and keep Mateo\'s at 16 by 9 since his are wide travel style shots.'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; The first thing I set is that this is a phone photo, not a studio shot. I\'ll keep the scene description short on purpose &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; because the more I describe the pose and the props, the more the model loses the face I locked in. So, [music] identity'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comes first and the staging barely gets a sentence. I\'ll also make sure the frame isn\'t perfectly composed because a clean centered shot is one of the biggest giveaways. [music] And I\'ll make sure they look like'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'ve actually been doing something instead of posing, [music] like giving them a little flush in the face or hair that isn\'t perfectly in place. The last thing I\'ll write in is the phone camera itself. So, only the face comes out [music] sharp, everything behind it goes'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'soft. For my character, I\'ll start at the gym standing at the rack between sets with just a light sweat sheen at the hairline and collarbones. Let\'s paste the prompt in, hit generate, and give it a quick check. And I really like'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how this one turned out because my character stayed looking exactly like me and the whole photo actually feels like someone snapped it while I was between sets. Then, a second one on the bench [music] with my character catching her'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'breath and this is where the sweat actually matters. I\'ll tell [music] it to keep the sweat as separate little beads sitting on top of the skin because the second it turns into a glossy film, it wipes out every pore underneath and'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'makes the whole thing look fake. I\'ll paste in the prompt, hit generate, and see if it turned out more realistic than what I\'ve got so far. And it looks even more real than the previous one because my character looks genuinely exhausted'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here and the hair turned out messy, which makes it look even more realistic. For May, she\'s in her bathroom halfway through a skin care step and the skin [music] texture is what really carries the whole prompt. So, I\'ll focus on'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'making the pores individually visible while keeping the serum sitting on top of the skin as separate [music] highlights instead of covering the whole face with it. Let\'s paste the prompt and run it through a next test then. And'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'still, [music] even though it\'s a beauty influencer, you can clearly see her face isn\'t too flawless because of the birthmarks and the serum on her right cheek, which makes it look believable. And the other is on the edge of her bed by the window talking about her routine.'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This one looks slightly cleaner than the previous one, but it still looks natural enough, so I\'ll keep it as is. Now, for Mateo, the lighting is doing most of the work. In the first image, he\'s [music] on a rooftop in the late afternoon, so'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll use hard unflattering light that rakes right across [music] his face. That way, every pore and crease creates its own little shadow. Soft flattering light would smooth all of that out, which is exactly what I don\'t want. And'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'since this one\'s outside, I\'ll let the frame [music] get messy. A cable running along the wall, a plastic chair knocked on its side, so it looks like a real snapshot nobody set up. I\'ll paste in the prompt, hit generate, and give it a'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'check. And he looks exactly as we\'ve pictured him with the character sheet, but the background looks incredibly good, too, especially with the hanging cables and the cracks in the walls. The last one is him sitting at a quiet'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'roadside cafe first thing in the morning with the same hard light coming in low. [music] Let\'s paste the prompt in and compare it to the previous one. And I feel like the lighting works even better here, but also, I like how the model'

[07:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'captured even these little imperfections on the table on the left. [music] By the end of this part, I\'ve got six images that look like someone just shot them with their [music] phone. But an image is still only a fraction of what the'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'viewer actually sees, so the final step is to actually animate them. I\'ll head over to the video tab on the left, [music] and in the add visual reference option, I\'ll drop one of my character\'s images into the reference box, which runs it as'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image-to-video, so it animates that exact photo instead of building something new. The part most people miss during generation is that even when a photo they use as a reference looks realistic, it can still turn out looking'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'too clean the second the face starts moving because the model re-renders the skin frame by frame and smooths it as it goes. So, I\'ll use the same texture instructions from the photo in the video prompt. The visible pores, sweat as'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'separate beads, and I\'ll tell it to keep it the whole way through. Now, the actual test. I\'ll write one prompt and send the same one to every model without changing a word right down to the camera note. So, the only thing different'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between the videos is the model itself. Every clip is 5 seconds, [music] one video per prompt. The five I\'m running this through in order are SeaArt 2.5, then SeaArt 2.0 fast, then Gemini 1.1'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'flash, then Grok Imagine 1.5, and last is Waifu 2.7. But, what\'s worth mentioning here is that the prompt will change sometimes when I\'m running the test through a particular model. All'

[08:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'five actually generate audio, and the only real difference is that SeaArt [music] it on or off, while the others just generate it and don\'t give me the switch. Waifu [music] 2.7 works differently again. It has a prompt'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'expansion toggle that\'s on by default [music] and rewrites whatever I paste in before it generates. The models also take different maximum file sizes, so I keep a smaller copy of each starting frame ready, and [music] their prompt'

[08:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'length limits are different, too. So, a prompt that runs fine in one can get rejected by another. And SeaArt 2.0 has two modes, fast and normal. For this test, we\'ll use fast mode, which can only generate 720p videos. So, I\'ll run'

[09:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the entire comparison at 720p. Otherwise, I\'d be testing the export setting instead of the model, and that\'s the only way the test stays fair. I\'ll run two kinds of video through all of'

[09:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this, and they break in different ways. One where the character says a single line to camera, so I can watch the lip sync and that tiny moment when each character gives a quick look somewhere on the side to make it look natural. And'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one with no talking at all, just the character breathing and pushing its hair back, which is honestly the harder one because with no dialogue to distract you, breathing that looks even slightly off jumps out immediately. [music] For'

[09:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the talking one, I\'ll give my gym character a throwaway line and I\'ll send that exact prompt through all five in that same order. Let\'s paste the prompts and walk through each tool to see which one handles [music] it the best.'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Two more sets and then I\'m done, I promise. &gt;&gt; Two more sets and then I\'m done, I promise. &gt;&gt; Two more sets and then I\'m done, I'

[10:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promise. &gt;&gt; Two more sets and then I\'m done, I promise. &gt;&gt; Two more sets and then I\'m done, I promise.'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; And for the clip of my character standing at the rack between sets, I can say Gemini handled it best because both the voice and the movement feel the most natural. My least favorite is 1 2.7 because my character moves quite'

[10:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unnaturally in it. Then I\'ll go with the second version where my character doesn\'t talk at all, but just catches a breath after a set. [music] I\'ll paste the prompts and run it through the same five again. &gt;&gt; [sighs and gasps]'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[sighs] [panting] [gasps] &gt;&gt; For the second one, I feel like both'

[11:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'SeaDance versions handled that one the best. While 1 2.7 still makes my character do some strange moves, like jerking her arm at one point. Now for May, I\'ll start with the one where she\'s sitting on the edge of her bed talking'

[11:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the camera about her routine and I\'ll run it through the same five. Let\'s paste the prompt in, hit [music] generate, and give it a quick check. &gt;&gt; I\'ve been using this for about 3 weeks now.'

[11:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; I\'ve been using this for about 3 weeks now. &gt;&gt; I\'ve been using this for about 3 weeks now. &gt;&gt; Um I\'ve been using this for about 3 weeks now.'

[11:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; I\'ve been using this for about 3 weeks now. &gt;&gt; And here Gemini handled it best because the flow of Mae\'s speech sounds smooth, and there\'s no awkward pause after she'

[12:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stops talking. Then the harder one, her skin [music] care step in the bathroom, because now she\'s patting product into her cheek. So there are fingers moving across the skin, and the texture has to survive under all that movement at once.'

[12:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The pores have to stay visible even under her fingertips, and the product has to keep sitting on top as separate highlights instead of smearing into a glossy layer as her hand moves. I\'ll run it through the same five in the same'

[12:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'order. [music] I\'ll paste in the prompt and hit generate to see how it goes. And for this one, I didn\'t spot many big'

[13:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'changes, but I feel like Cidence 2.0 made it the best in terms of visuals, and the sound matched what was happening on the screen most accurately. Then I\'ll go with Mateo\'s videos, and the thing [music] is they carry one extra'

[13:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'requirement none of the others do. Because for these, the light has to stay hard the entire time. [music] So in his prompt, I\'ll lock that hard raking light in place, and block any soft fill from creeping in mid-video. Because instead'

[13:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of asking generic questions whether his skin looks good, I\'ll just watch whether the sharp shadow on his face holds up through 5 seconds of movement. And I\'ll put his through the same five in the same order. Let\'s paste the prompt in,'

[13:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hit generate, and check the first one. &gt;&gt; Third day here and I still haven\'t figured out the bus. Third day here and I still haven\'t figured out the bus.'

[13:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Third day here and I still haven\'t figured out the bus. Third day here and I still haven\'t figured out the bus. Third day here and I still haven\'t'

[14:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'figured out the bus. &gt;&gt; And for this one, Gemini is the clear winner to me because the way Matteo moves looks very smooth and natural along with his speech and how he acts throughout the whole shot. Now, as the'

[14:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'last one, I\'ll go with the second one of him sitting at the cafe. So, I\'ll paste the prompt and generate [music] it to see if it turns out looking even better. And here I feel like Seedens 2.5 handled'

[14:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it best because in this one Matteo actually takes a full sip instead of just putting the glass [music] to his mouth like in the other videos. By now, you\'ve seen my exact process for making ultra-realistic influencers and how the'

[15:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'main models actually hold up on each type of video so you don\'t burn credits testing to find the right one. And even with this workflow, doing it across a separate [music] platform for every model will take you way longer to get a'

[15:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality result. Luckily, inside OpenArt you get access to [music] every tool I\'ve used today, so you can run the whole process from scratch in one place. So, sign up to OpenArt with my link in'

[15:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the description and start building your own ultra-realistic AI influencers [music] today. Thanks for watching, and I\'ll see you in the next one.'
